Autumnal life in this district obliges man to constant purposes and provision for approaching winter. Laborious as summer seems, with its unceasing toil in field and garden, yet the harvest reaped is begotten of summer's own growth. Winter demands preparation during this labor; for all must be gathered, stored, secured to meet the stern season, unfriendly to man's wants, nearer intruding with each daily-falling leaf. Is it not fitting, then, that each genial night under stars calm and cold, succeeding days honest and effortful, be spent by hearty laborers in stocking surplus against need?

